          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have asthma and I use a blue inhaler.",
          "answer": "A reliever inhaler is the one taken for quick relief when asthma symptoms flare, such as wheeze, chest tightness or cough. The asthma summary in this corpus describes a reliever as one part of asthma management, alongside a regular controller inhaler, trigger avoidance, technique checks and a written action plan. Your question doesn't say which inhaler you have, and the passages I found don't cover colour coding, so check the label or ask your pharmacist what your inhaler is for.\n\n**Using it safely:**\n- Use it exactly as your prescriber or action plan describes. Don't adjust the dose yourself.\n- Good technique matters. The adherence guidance notes that poor technique is common and often the first thing reviewed when treatment seems to fail. A spacer, if you have one, helps with pressurized inhalers. Ask for your technique to be checked at a review.\n- If you use more than one type of inhaler, ask for each one's technique to be checked, because the breathing instructions can differ.\n\n**When regular use may mean review:**\nThe passages don't give a set number of puffs per week that counts as \"too often.\" They do say that a review should happen one to three months after starting or changing treatment, then every three to twelve months, and within one week after an exacerbation (a flare-up). If you find you need your reliever often, that is a good reason to book a review sooner rather than later.\n\n**Seek emergency help straight away** if you have any of these: difficulty speaking in full sentences, breathlessness at rest, drowsiness or confusion, blue lips or fingers, or an attack that doesn't improve after reliever treatment or comes back within a few hours. The corpus also notes that a chest that has gone quiet during a severe attack is a serious sign, not a reassuring one.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
